$CATEGORY: Q15 - Immutable Objects

::Q1501::
A way of organizing code and data into objects, rather than independent methods.{
    ~ object
    = object-oriented
}

::Q1502::
A collection of related data that comes with a set of methods that operate on the data.{
    = object
    ~ object-oriented
}

::Q1503::
primitive - A data type that stores a single value and provides no methods. {TRUE}

::Q1504::
Mutable objects can be modified once created. Strings are not mutable objects. {TRUE}

::Q1505::
BigIntegers objects are immutables, meaning that methods like add, multiply... {
    = return new BigIntegers objects
    ~ modify the existing object
}

::Q1506::
Wrapper classes provide constants and methods for working with primitive types. {TRUE}

::Q1507::
Parse means to read a string and interpret or translate it. Is Integer.parseInt("five") correct? {FALSE}

::Q1508::
A string with no elements and a length of zero - {
    = empty string
    ~ null string
}
    
::Q1509::
A string that does not reference to any object - {
    ~ empty string
    = null string
}

::Q1510::
To determine what methods a class should have {
    = design process
    ~ encapsulate
}

::Q1511::
To wrap data or methods inside an object {
    ~ design process
    = encapsulate
}

::Q1512::
To replace something unnecessarily specific (like a constant value) with something appropriately general (like a parameter).{
    = refactor
    = generalize
    = generalise
}